values ((select id from public.teams limit 1), $il$i083$il$, $il$Put the company facts on the review card so a draft can be judged at a glance$il$, $il$Outreach & drafting$il$, $il$Both$il$, $il$must$il$, $il$open$il$, $il$Oliver$il$, '2026-09-16T10:03:19.000Z'::timestamptz, 4, '{}'::text[], '{}'::text[], null, null, false, false, $il${}$il$::jsonb,
$il$The tiles under the contact name work well: free DM 0 credits, 2nd degree, message 1 of 3, DE, profile updated 1d ago. They are all about the SEND. Nothing on the card is about the COMPANY, which is what actually decides whether the message is right.

Add a second row of tiles carrying the company facts:
  Insurance state: greenfield, monthly, or yearly
  The incumbent by name, where there is one
  The wedge, in two or three words
  Research stage, and whether it was the agent or a person, per i079
  The score, and its confidence
  Physical stores, yes or no
  A group flag where the company belongs to one with a live deal

Row one is about the send. Row two is about the company. Then approve, edit or reject can be decided from the top of the card instead of by opening the company in another tab.$il$,
$il$MOST OF THIS IS ALREADY ON THE CARD, JUST NOT SKIMMABLE, which is worth Brad knowing before he builds anything new. Why this now on the Robert Karl draft reads: cold open to a P0 whale operator with a live competitor, bolttech, on the book; anchors on publicly visible product detail to prove research was done; asks a discovery question rather than pitching, since this is a displacement play against an incumbent. That paragraph contains the insurance state, the incumbent, the wedge and the strategy. It is genuinely good. It is also four lines of prose, and Oliver is reviewing dozens.
So this is as much about form as content: keep the paragraph, and lift the four or five facts out of it into tiles above it.

COLOUR ONLY THE ONES THAT SHOULD STOP YOU. If every tile looks the same they all get skipped. Neutral for context, amber for check this, red for do not send. A group collision or an unresearched company should be visibly different from DE or 2nd degree.

BILLING SHAPE IS THE ONE PEOPLE FORGET. Greenfield and yearly incumbents are the priority; monthly across the whole range is deprioritised, not excluded. That is a ranking rule already in use, and it is invisible on this card today even though it changes whether the message is worth a credit at all.

WHAT NOT TO ADD. Anything that does not change the approve, edit or reject decision stays on the company record. The test for a tile is whether Oliver would act differently if it said something else. Revenue and employee count fail that test; insurance state passes it.$il$);

