insert into public.improvements (team_id, item_key, title, category, owner, priority, status, raised_by, created_at, build_wave, blocks, blocked_by, done_by, done_at, pinned, hidden, seen_by, detail, notes)
values ((select id from public.teams limit 1), $il$i085$il$, $il$The tabs and the Draft Status filter are two status filters that silently cancel each other$il$, $il$Lovable UI$il$, $il$Brad$il$, $il$must$il$, $il$open$il$, $il$Oliver$il$, '2026-09-16T10:18:32.000Z'::timestamptz, 5, '{}'::text[], '{}'::text[], null, null, false, false, $il${}$il$::jsonb,
$il$Oliver approved two drafts, then filtered Draft Status to Approved and got No outreach touches found. He reasonably concluded the approvals had been lost.

They were not. The tab bar reads Approved 3, so they saved correctly.

THE LIKELY CAUSE IS NOT A BROKEN FILTER. The tabs ARE a status filter. Pending Review was still the selected tab while Draft Status was set to Approved, and a touch cannot be both. The empty result is arithmetically correct and completely unhelpful, because nothing on the screen says the two controls are fighting.

FIX THE CONTRADICTION, NOT THE FILTER. Either selecting a Draft Status moves the tab to match, or the Draft Status control disappears while a status tab is active, or the empty state says why: no results, because the Pending Review tab and the Approved filter cannot both be true.

No outreach touches found is the wrong message for a contradiction. It reads as the data is missing, which is exactly the conclusion Oliver drew, and with approvals it is an alarming one.$il$,
$il$THIS IS THE SAME SHAPE AS i041, two controls over one piece of state that do not agree. There the header chip and the field below it disagreed about Status. Here the tab and the dropdown disagree about Draft Status. Worth fixing as one habit rather than two bugs: one fact, one control, and where a second view of it exists it reflects rather than competes.

AN EMPTY LIST NEEDS A REASON, ALWAYS. Distinguish nothing matches these filters, which invites clearing them, from nothing exists here, which does not. The current message says neither and implies the worse one.

THE APPROVED PILE IS ABOUT TO GROW, and this matters more than usual this week. Send is broken, see i082, so approving does not send. Oliver said he is going to work through and approve more now. That means a backlog of approved-but-unsent drafts accumulating behind a broken send, in a view he currently cannot filter to. Two things follow:
  Make sure the Approved view is easy to reach and shows how long each has been sitting.
  When send is fixed, there has to be a way to work that backlog, ideally sending from the Approved list rather than reopening each card.

AND APPROVED DRAFTS GO STALE. A message approved today and sent four days later can reference something that has moved, and the day-7 reply check timing is computed from the send, not the approval. Worth showing the age of an approved draft and flagging anything approved more than a couple of days ago for a re-read before it goes.$il$);

insert into public.improvements (team_id, item_key, title, category, owner, priority, status, raised_by, created_at, build_wave, blocks, blocked_by, done_by, done_at, pinned, hidden, seen_by, detail, notes)
values ((select id from public.teams limit 1), $il$i086$il$, $il$Settle what Refurbished and SIM-free mean, because the data already answers it differently$il$, $il$Data & schema$il$, $il$Both$il$, $il$must$il$, $il$open$il$, $il$Oliver$il$, '2026-09-16T10:34:56.000Z'::timestamptz, 1, '{}'::text[], '{}'::text[], null, null, false, false, $il${}$il$::jsonb,
$il$Oliver asked to confirm the two fields. His reading: refurbished Yes means they sell refurbished; SIM-free Yes means they also sell NEW phones without a contract.

The first half is right. The second half is not how the field has been filled, and the lake proves it.

SIM-FREE MEANS CONTRACT-FREE, NOT NEW. In retail it describes how a handset is sold, outright and not tied to a network, and it applies to refurbished devices just as much as new ones. A refurbished iPhone bought outright is SIM-free.

THE NUMBERS SETTLE IT. Across the lake: 68 companies are Yes and Yes, 54 are No refurbished and Yes SIM-free, 14 are No and No, and only THREE are Yes refurbished and No SIM-free. Under Oliver's reading every pure refurbisher would sit in that last bucket, and there are 313 companies whose category is Refurbished Specialist. They are not there. They are in Yes and Yes, because whoever filled the field used the standard meaning.

So if the definition changes now, 68 or more existing records become wrong silently. Worth deciding deliberately rather than by drift.$il$,
$il$NEITHER DEFINITION IS QUITE WHAT PIER NEEDS, which is the more useful conclusion. The question that actually decides whether Pier Protect fits is not new versus refurbished. It is HOW THE DEVICE IS SOLD:
  Bought outright by the customer, new or refurbished. The core case. The customer owns it, so protection can be offered after the sale.
  Sold on an operator contract, billed by the telco. A different commercial route, and the reason A1, Drei, spusu and Vodafone are a separate kind of conversation.
  Rented or subscribed, where the customer never owns it. Grover. A different model again, and the reason the Grover wedge is a programme sale rather than a checkout attach.

THE THIRD STATE IS ALREADY HIDING IN ANOTHER FIELD. Category carries Device rental / subscription (Grover model) for 15 companies. So the commercial model is already being recorded, just in the wrong place and only for the rental case.

SUGGESTED SHAPE, to argue with: two questions instead of two booleans.
  CONDITION SOLD: new, refurbished, or both.
  HOW SOLD: outright, on operator contract, rental or subscription. Multi select, since large retailers do more than one.
That answers Oliver's original question cleanly, keeps the 68 existing Yes and Yes records meaningful, and pulls the Grover case out of Category where it does not belong.

THIS IS THE i078 TEST FAILING IN PUBLIC. That item says a field needing a paragraph of explanation is usually two fields. Two people looked at SIM-free and read it differently, and one record in the lake has SIM-free filled in with the word refurbished inside it, which is somebody conflating them at the point of entry. Whatever is decided, it goes in the field help.

MIGRATION NOTE: three records are currently Yes refurbished and No SIM-free. Those three are worth looking at by hand whichever way this goes, because under either definition they are unusual.$il$);

insert into public.improvements (team_id, item_key, title, category, owner, priority, status, raised_by, created_at, build_wave, blocks, blocked_by, done_by, done_at, pinned, hidden, seen_by, detail, notes)
values ((select id from public.teams limit 1), $il$i087$il$, $il$Deep link straight to a Lovable record from the logs and from Claude$il$, $il$Integrations$il$, $il$Both$il$, $il$nice$il$, $il$open$il$, $il$Oliver$il$, '2026-09-16T11:55:34.000Z'::timestamptz, 6, '{}'::text[], '{}'::text[], null, null, false, false, $il${}$il$::jsonb,
$il$Oliver asked for a Lovable MCP so records can be linked from artifacts. The underlying want is deep links: an entry in this log or in Intel Staging that points at the actual company or contact, one click away, instead of naming it in prose.

THAT PROBABLY DOES NOT NEED A LOVABLE MCP. It needs two things, and one of them already exists.
  The record id, which comes from Supabase, read only. That is the connector in i001.
  The URL pattern, which is already observable from the browser.

Observed patterns so far:
  pier-lead-lake.lovable.app/contacts/<uuid>?tab=overview
  pier-lead-lake.lovable.app/outreach/<uuid>
  pier-lead-lake.lovable.app/reconciliation?tab=replies
The companies pattern has not been confirmed yet and should be, along with whether a tab can be targeted directly, for instance straight to Conversation.

So this is mostly waiting on i001 rather than on a new tool. Low priority, as Oliver said, but it gets close to free once the connector works.$il$,
$il$THE PAYOFF IS IN INTEL STAGING MORE THAN HERE. Those entries name a company and then describe which fields should change. With a deep link, each one becomes a click through to the record being corrected, which is the difference between a list of findings and a work queue. The Save Group and asgoodasnew entry, the three spusu records, the Patrick Bruecker reconciliation, all of them currently make the reader go and find the record by hand.

OLIVER'S WRITE INSTINCT MATCHES WHAT IS ALREADY BUILT, worth confirming rather than re-deciding. Nothing gets entered from the Lovable side by an agent; writes go through Supabase under the write guard, which runs proposals into a Data Review Board, waits for Oliver's approval, then applies one primary key scoped statement at a time. That is the design as of 16 September and it is at its most cautious setting. So read only linking is exactly the right scope for this item.

IF A LOVABLE MCP DOES EXIST, the question to ask before adopting it is what it gives that Supabase does not. Supabase already has the data. A Lovable layer would mostly add app concepts, routes and views, which matter for links but are simpler to hard code as a URL pattern than to depend on another connector, another auth and another thing that can return unauthorized. Given this week, that last point is not theoretical.

WORTH DOING WHEN IT LANDS: put the record link on the item itself in both logs, as a small button, rather than pasting URLs into the text where they age badly and cannot be checked.$il$);

insert into public.improvements (team_id, item_key, title, category, owner, priority, status, raised_by, created_at, build_wave, blocks, blocked_by, done_by, done_at, pinned, hidden, seen_by, detail, notes)
values ((select id from public.teams limit 1), $il$i088$il$, $il$A New message button, so Oliver can start a conversation himself$il$, $il$Outreach & drafting$il$, $il$Both$il$, $il$must$il$, $il$open$il$, $il$Oliver$il$, '2026-09-16T12:31:58.000Z'::timestamptz, 3, '{}'::text[], array[$il$i052$il$]::text[], null, null, false, false, $il${}$il$::jsonb,
$il$Everything Lovable sends today comes out of a sequence. There is no way for Oliver to simply message someone because he has decided to. Add a New message button on the contact, with a channel choice: LinkedIn DM, InMail, or email.

It should let him write it himself OR generate a draft. He must be able to type two lines without waiting for a model.

THE GUARDS STILL RUN. This is the obvious way to build a hole in the side of the system: a manual send button is exactly the route that skips the cooldown, the do not contact flag, the Monday suppression, the group collision check and the daily connection request budget. So the button runs the same gate as everything else, shows what the gate says, and allows an override only with a reason and only by Oliver.

Same channel rules as i052: connected means a free DM, not connected means an InMail and a credit, email only where the market allows it. Preselect the right one rather than offering four equal options.

And it is a touch like any other. Written at the moment of sending, and it gets a follow up, defaulting to automatic per i063.$il$,
$il$THE SEQUENCE COUNTER IS THE TRAP. An off sequence message has to not corrupt message 1 of 3. Decide explicitly whether a manual send advances the sequence, sits outside it, or starts a new one, and show which on the card. This matters more than it sounds because the counter is already wrong in at least one place: a draft on 16 September read logged as chaser 3 of 2, which is i053. Adding an untyped manual touch on top of that makes the position meaningless.

WHAT IT IS ACTUALLY FOR, worth writing down so the design serves it. Oliver has just met someone at an event. Someone has been recommended by a partner. A company has posted something that makes today the day. A reply came in somewhere else and the thread needs restarting. None of those are sequence events, and all of them are the highest value messages he sends, because they carry a real reason.

SO THE WHY THIS NOW FIELD SHOULD BE WRITABLE HERE. For a sequenced message the system explains itself. For a manual one, Oliver knows the reason and nothing else does. Capturing it in one line at the point of sending is what keeps the record readable in three months, and it is the input for the follow up logic deciding what to do when there is no reply.

IT SHARES EVERYTHING WITH i052 EXCEPT THE STARTING POINT. Same composer, same channel selector, same guard check, same touch write, same follow up setter. One is replying inside a thread, the other is opening one. Build the composer once.

ONE LIMIT WORTH KEEPING: one person at a time. A New message button that can be pointed at a filtered list is a bulk sender, and that is a different thing with a different risk profile, especially while the LinkedIn account is throttled. See i071 and i019.$il$);

insert into public.improvements (team_id, item_key, title, category, owner, priority, status, raised_by, created_at, build_wave, blocks, blocked_by, done_by, done_at, pinned, hidden, seen_by, detail, notes)
values ((select id from public.teams limit 1), $il$i089$il$, $il$Hard block on cold email where it is not permitted, with a recorded consent exception$il$, $il$Guards$il$, $il$Both$il$, $il$must$il$, $il$open$il$, $il$Oliver$il$, '2026-09-16T12:32:55.000Z'::timestamptz, 2, '{}'::text[], array[$il$i005$il$]::text[], null, null, false, false, $il${}$il$::jsonb,
$il$Cold email is not permitted in several of our markets. The system needs to refuse it rather than warn about it, with one exception: where the contact has opted in or agreed, email is allowed and the agreement is recorded.

Oliver is right that the rules still need defining. That is the first task, and it is a decision rather than a build.

THE WORKING RULE TODAY, for reference and to be confirmed rather than assumed: cold email is permitted for France, Belgium, Netherlands and Switzerland-targeted contacts. Germany, Austria, Spain, Luxembourg and Czechia are LinkedIn led. Italy is recorded inconsistently in our own notes, listed as LinkedIn led in one place and as unruled in another, so it needs settling explicitly.

IT READS THE CONTACT'S COUNTRY, NOT THE COMPANY'S. Coolblue is the worked example: Dutch company, German country manager. Company country would clear the email. Contact country stops it. Same point as i038 and i054.

FAIL CLOSED. Unknown country means no email. An unknown must never default to permitted on a control like this.

Oliver flagged it as for later. I have put it as a must, because the email channel is being built now in i026 and i052, and a compliance block designed in is a different job from one retrofitted onto a live channel. Knock it back if you disagree.$il$,
$il$THE CARVE OUT THAT MATTERS MOST: A REPLY IS NOT COLD EMAIL. Someone who emails us first has opened the conversation, and replying is plainly legitimate. A naive country block would have stopped today's reply to Zuzanna Nowak at refurbed, who is in Austria and wrote to Oliver. If the guard cannot tell an inbound thread from a cold approach it will block the most valuable email we send. So the rule is about INITIATING, and the check is whether they contacted us first.

CONSENT NEEDS ITS OWN RECORD, not a tick box. If email is allowed because someone agreed, the record has to say when, how, and where the evidence is. Verbally on a call, in writing in a thread, through a form, at an event. A bare consent equals yes field is worth very little if it is ever questioned, and it is the kind of thing that gets questioned precisely when it matters.

QUESTIONS THE DEFINITION HAS TO ANSWER, worth listing so the conversation is short:
  Which countries, confirmed rather than inherited from a note.
  Does an existing business relationship count as a basis, since several jurisdictions allow a soft opt in to an existing customer, and we are not their supplier.
  Does a B2B business address change anything, since some regimes treat business contacts differently from consumers.
  Does a personal email at a company domain differ from a role address like info at.
  How long consent lasts, and whether it survives the person changing employer.

THIS IS A LEGAL QUESTION WITH A PRODUCT IMPLEMENTATION, not the reverse. Worth someone qualified confirming the country list and the consent basis before it is coded, because getting it wrong is a regulator matter rather than a wasted message. Oliver and Brad should agree the mechanism; somebody else should sign off the rule.

THE UNSUBSCRIBE SIDE COMES WITH IT. Whatever is built has to honour an opt out immediately and permanently, which the lake already models as status Opted out plus the do not contact flag with the next action cleared. Lovable needs the same, and it needs to be the one thing no override can get past.$il$);

