values ((select id from public.teams limit 1), $il$i090$il$, $il$Monday-only contacts can never be matched, so their messages recur in the queue forever$il$, $il$Integrations$il$, $il$Brad$il$, $il$must$il$, $il$open$il$, $il$Claude$il$, '2026-09-16T12:36:07.000Z'::timestamptz, 2, '{}'::text[], '{}'::text[], null, null, false, false, $il${}$il$::jsonb,
$il$Oliver asked why Patrick Bruecker is not in Lovable. The answer: he is not in the lake either. Searched on 9 September across Contacts and Companies, there is no Bruecker under any spelling. He exists only in monday, on the asgoodasnew account.

That is not an import bug. asgoodasnew is C290, a live monday deal, and its contacts are deliberately blocked from the lake with the note worked in Monday, not the Lake. Patrick was never a lake contact, so there was nothing for Lovable to receive.

THE PROBLEM IS WHAT HAPPENS NEXT. Every message to or from a monday-only contact arrives in Reconciliation with nothing to match it to. Oliver dismisses it, and the next one appears. There is no state in which the queue is clean, because the matching target does not exist by design.

Two ways out, and it is worth choosing rather than drifting:
  Import monday contacts as suppressed, read only records purely so messages can attach to them, clearly marked worked in monday and excluded from every worklist, draft and count.
  Or teach Reconciliation to recognise a monday company and route the message there with a label, instead of offering it for assignment to a Growth Engine contact.

The first keeps the message history complete in one place. The second is less work. Either beats the current state, which is a queue that cannot be emptied.$il$,
$il$THIS IS THE SECOND HALF OF i044. That item is about monday suppression being applied inconsistently across screens. This is the case where suppression is working correctly at the company level and still produces a permanently dirty queue, because the contact does not exist to be suppressed. Worth fixing together.

IT IS NOT ONE CONTACT. Every contact on every monday deal is in this position, and monday deals are the accounts with the most conversation, because they are the ones actually progressing. So the volume of unmatchable messages grows with success, which is the wrong way round.

THE REAL QUESTION UNDERNEATH: WHERE DOES A MONDAY CONVERSATION LIVE. Today a message to Patrick sits in LinkedIn, is picked up by the watcher, arrives in Lovable with nowhere to go, and the deal it belongs to is in monday. Three systems, no owner. The clean sheet answer in i069 is one system; until then, at minimum decide which of the three is supposed to hold it so the other two can stop asking.

AND IT AFFECTS THE NUMBERS. Unmatched messages from monday contacts inflate the reconciliation count and depress the apparent match rate, so neither figure means what it looks like while this is unresolved.$il$);

insert into public.improvements (team_id, item_key, title, category, owner, priority, status, raised_by, created_at, build_wave, blocks, blocked_by, done_by, done_at, pinned, hidden, seen_by, detail, notes)
values ((select id from public.teams limit 1), $il$i091$il$, $il$Companies shows page 1 of 23 with no way to turn the page$il$, $il$Lovable UI$il$, $il$Brad$il$, $il$nice$il$, $il$open$il$, $il$Oliver$il$, '2026-09-16T12:36:36.000Z'::timestamptz, 5, '{}'::text[], '{}'::text[], null, null, false, false, $il${}$il$::jsonb,
$il$The Companies list ends at the bottom of the first page. The footer reads 1,137 companies, page 1 of 23, so the pagination exists and knows there are 22 more, but there is nothing to click.

Contacts has Previous and Next buttons in the same footer. Companies does not, or they are not visible.

ONE CHEAP THING TO CHECK FIRST: the Edit with Lovable badge sits in the bottom right corner, which on Contacts is exactly where the Previous and Next buttons are. They may be there and covered rather than missing. Worth looking before building anything.

Oliver marked this very low priority and it is.$il$,
$il$PAGINATION IS NOT REALLY THE ANSWER HERE, which is why the low priority is right. Twenty three pages of 1,137 companies in no useful order is not something anyone pages through. Nobody is going to click Next twenty times looking for a company. What makes this list usable is the ranked, score sorted view in i071, plus search and filters. Once those exist, page 2 is somewhere you almost never need to go.

SO IF IT IS A QUICK FIX, DO IT, and if it is not, it can wait behind i071. The only case where paging genuinely matters is working systematically through a filtered set, for instance every company that is Untouched in Germany, and that is a case where the filter has already cut 1,137 down to something small.

WHILE IN THERE, the same footer inconsistency is worth a glance across the other lists. Contacts has the controls, Companies appears not to, and an interface where the same footer behaves differently in two places is the kind of thing that makes someone doubt the whole screen.$il$);

